we've covered the healthcare systems of  Singapore Germany the United Kingdom  Canada and a lot of other systems  Switzerland and France which have  slightly different health care systems  and there's a really interesting case  study which is that of Taiwan which  someone on our subreddit mentioned and  I've been researching for the last  couple of days the Ministry of Health  and Welfare runs health care in Taiwan  meaning that health insurance is  government-run and the current system  was put in place in 1995 so it's sort of  recent compared to other systems that  countries have that have been around  much much longer and as of 10 years ago  Taiwan had about 99 percent health care  coverage with their system and the  health care premiums are paid through  the payroll tax shared by employees and  employers and that is actually similar  to the United States there's a split  employers pay part of it and employees  pay part of it but it goes towards a  government-run single-payer insurance  system rather than each company sort of  figuring out which for-profit health  insurance carrier than what they want to  use it's heavily subsidized so if you  can't pay because of poverty if you're a  veteran there are no financial barriers  to ultimately getting the care that you  need  under the Taiwanese system but the  providers are still mostly private in  other words the the health care  providers are not government-run such as  through a national health care system  like what we see in the United Kingdom  and there have been significant  reductions to the disparities in health  outcomes between the rich and the poor  or the haves and have-nots since this  system was implemented very low spending  per person in Taiwan long life  expectancy in Taiwan low infant  mortality rate in Taiwan and the  question is that what when we consider  all of the different proposals that  exist in the United States single-payer  nationalizing health care all of these  different possibilities what I think is  really important to understand and it's  sort of depressing to have to under  sandesh I'm not married to one  particular way of expanding healthcare  coverage to everybody in the United  States Medicare currently has some  issues so while Medicare for all would  be way better than the system we have it  doesn't have to be the one solution but  all of the systems that these modern  countries have implemented they all are  better than the system we have in the  United States they are systems with far  less waste  they are systems that cost much less to  get the health care people need to them  on a per capita basis and they are  systems that increase access as a  percentage of the population some of the  critiques we hear when universal  coverage systems are put in place  include if all of a sudden everybody has  coverage and they're not paying for  individual services at the point of sale  people are going to overuse their health  insurance and get procedures that they  don't need that's absolutely ridiculous  and what we have seen in Taiwan is that  yes healthcare usage has increased  because everybody or all but one percent  of the population now have health  coverage but what that means is when  people have symptoms they go to the  doctor when it's time for your annual  checkup people go although in modern  United States health care law those  annual checkups have to be offered a  copay free so the right likes to pretend  that if people don't have to pay for  each service they get they're going to  overuse health care which is laughably  ridiculous getting health care services  isn't a hobby and people don't go out  and just get procedures that they don't  need nor do doctors perform procedures  that are not medically necessary  you'll get preventive care that helps  prevent expensive treatments in the  future by virtue of preventing disease  and the biggest problem that Taiwan is  having is actually that they don't have  enough doctors and nurses relative to  the size of the population but still  sadly by sort of overworking the doctors  and nurses that they have they've  managed to have relatively few wait time  issues but it's not great to have to  overwork your doctors and  nurses this reminds me of the bazaar  exchange during Bernie Sanders Town Hall  on Monday with Martha McCallum and Bret  Baier where Martha McCallum said  someone's paying for it either way  whether it's paid through deductions  from your paycheck or whether it's paid  through taxes or whether it's from your  employer or not someone's paying for it  either way and this is sort of exactly  the point health care is not free money  doesn't grow on trees either way we're  paying for it  why don't we eliminate many of the cost  inflationary mechanisms in the system we  have medical billing code coders who  work for the providers medical billing  coders who work for the insurers health  insurance fraud investigators management  consultants for insurance companies who  are looking to make money the profit  which is only two or three percent for  insurance companies but still adds up if  either way someone is paying for it why  don't we get rid of all the stuff that  we don't really need pay less and get  more someone's paying for it either way  let's pay less isn't that the obvious  direction we should be going and Taiwan  is just again one of another of the  dozen or 15 or maybe even 20 examples  around the world that are all slightly  different from each other but all better  and more effective than in the United  States if you love learning new things  you probably want to check this out the  david pakman show today is sponsored by  skill share and we're giving you two  entire months of free access to skill  share when you click the link underneath  this video skill share is a vibrant  online learning community offering more  than 25,000 video classes on just about  anything you can think of get better at  playing an instrument learn to u